# Commit message quality — commits are evidence, not labels

**Scope:** every agent-authored commit. Promoted 12 Aug 2026 from a section inside
`git-commit-hygiene-shared-worktree.md` to its own rule, because it kept being violated while
buried in a document about *which files* to commit.

## Why this matters

`openspec/changes/README.md` makes `status: implemented` mean "shipped to `dev`", and the check for
that is `git log --oneline -- <paths the change touches>`. **That check is only as good as the
commit subjects it returns.** A history of:

```
On dev: codex-before-origin-dev-pull-2026-07-03
index on dev: 7af508fd
Checking in the code to the fixing of 1st july removal.
Saving agency monthly report.
Files to be added
checking in the files with the buttons and fixes.
checking in the files.
```

…returns hits without evidence. You cannot tell from any of them whether the change shipped, what
it did, or which requirement it satisfied — so an `implemented` claim resting on them is
unfalsifiable, which is the same thing as unsupported.

This keeps happening. The C337 audit (4 Aug 2026) found seven such commits; `2e6465f4` and
`cda89ba5` were added on 12 Aug 2026 while C405 was in flight. **None are rewritten** — `dev` is
shared and a rebase would do more damage than the drift. The rule binds going forward.

## The rules

1. **Never commit an autostash or index-state message.** `On <branch>: …` and
   `index on <branch>: …` are `git stash` artifacts. If one is about to become a commit, you have
   stashed when you meant to commit, or resolved a stash conflict by committing the stash entry
   itself. Stop and redo it.
2. **Never use a progress-note subject.** "Checking in the code", "Saving …", "Files to be added",
   "Other files", "more fixes", "checking in the files" describe *your session*, not *the change*.
   Six months later they are indistinguishable from noise.
3. **A commit satisfying an OpenSpec task must name it** — `C405`, `T405.09`, `issue-173` — in the
   subject or body. That is what makes `git log --oneline -- <path>` an audit rather than a list of
   timestamps.
4. **The subject must survive losing its diff.** If someone reads only the subject and cannot say
   what changed and why, rewrite it before committing — not after.
5. **The body carries the evidence.** For anything non-trivial: what was wrong, what you changed,
   and how you know it works (test counts, measured numbers). This is where a reviewer looks when
   the subject is not enough.
6. **Fix your own subject before committing.** You cannot rewrite shared history, so the only
   opportunity is before the commit exists. There is no cleanup pass later.

## Shape

```
<type>(<scope>): <what changed, imperative, survives losing the diff>

<why it was wrong / what the change does>
<evidence: test counts, measurements, commands run>
<anything deliberately not done, and why>

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

Good (C405): `perf(C405): a167 agency_id indexes + collapse clients-page N+1 (round 2 hygiene)`
Bad (same day, another session): `checking in the files.`

## Automated backstop — advisory by design

`scripts/audit_openspec_drift.py` check **C07** warns on subjects matching
`^(On|index on) \w+:|^(Checking|Saving|Files) `.

It is **advisory and never fails the run**, deliberately: the existing history is immutable, and a
permanently-red check is one people learn to skip. It exists to catch the next one at review time,
not to relitigate the last nine. **Do not wire it into a deploy gate** — that would create pressure
to weaken it.

Note C07's pattern is case-sensitive and anchored, so a lowercase `checking in the files.` slips
past it. Treat the rule, not the script, as the authority.

